input points on the convex hull. The hull points are ordered counterclockwise.","delaunay.coords":"This property contains the input coordinates as a flat array of alternating x and y values. It preserves the constructor's array type or uses Float64Array when created through the point-based factory.","delaunay.update()":"This method recomputes the triangulation after coordinate values are modified in place, avoiding expensive memory allocations. It is useful for iterative relaxation algorithms such as Lloyd's algorithm.","Performance":"The section compares Delaunator with seven other JavaScript triangulation libraries across uniform, Gaussian, grid, and degenerate datasets containing 100,000 or one million points. Delaunator records the fastest time in every listed benchmark, while some competitors time out or exhaust memory.","Papers":"The algorithm draws on three papers concerning sweep-line, radial sweep-hull, and circle-sweep Delaunay triangulation techniques. These references were published between 2010 and 2013 and include links and author credits.","Robustness":"Delaunator is intended to produce valid output even for highly degenerate input. It relies on robust-predicates, a modern port of Jonathan Shewchuk's industry-standard robust geometric predicates.","Ports to other languages":"The README lists ports for Rust, Go, C++, C#, Ruby, Python, Python/Torch, Kotlin, Java, Stata/Mata, and Julia. Each entry links to its corresponding project repository."},"concerns":[],"rationale":"Read README.md and summarized every heading-defined section, including API subsections, in two sentences each while excluding Example as assigned."}

