{"sections":{"oniguruma-parser 🌿":"oniguruma-parser is a TypeScript library for parsing, validating, traversing, transforming, and optimizing Oniguruma regular expressions. It has been battle-tested through Oniguruma-To-ES and tm-grammars, which Shiki uses to process tens of thousands of real-world patterns.","📜 Contents":"The contents list links to installation, AST parsing, traversal and transformation, pattern generation, and regex optimization. It also links to known differences and the supported Oniguruma version.","Additional modules":"Separate parser, traverser, generator, and optimizer modules supplement the root export. They provide AST construction and parsing utilities, traversal and transformation, pattern and flag generation, and regex optimization.","🌲 Convert a pattern to an AST":"The toOnigurumaAst function parses a pattern into an OnigurumaAst, accepting optional flags and captureGroup or singleline rules. It rejects invalid patterns or flags and wraps the parser's parse function with appropriate Unicode property validation data.","🌀 Traverse and transform an AST":"The traverser module supports traversing and transforming an OnigurumaAst. This section directs readers to that module's README for details and examples.","↩️ Convert an AST to a pattern":"The generator module converts an OnigurumaAst into pattern and flags strings. This section links to the generator README for details and examples.","🪄 Optimize regexes":"The optimizer can minify regexes and improve their performance and readability while preserving exactly which strings they match. The section demonstrates a simplified pattern and links to the optimizer documentation and an interactive demo.","🆚 Known differences":"The library documents unsupported features and behavioral differences from Oniguruma. These known differences are intended to be resolved in future versions.","Unsupported features":"Unsupported constructs include certain character escapes, code point sequences, absence constructs, conditionals, non-built-in callouts, some backreferences, and certain flag placements, but the library supports more than 99.99% of patterns in a roughly 55,000-pattern TextMate sample. Expandable notes explain numbered forward backreferences, missing validation errors, and planned CharacterSequence, Conditional, and Callout AST node types.","Behavior differences":"Behavioral differences concern long unenclosed backreferences and edge cases involving Oniguruma bugs or undefined behavior. The following subsections describe the library's current handling and some planned compatibility improvements.","Unenclosed four-digit backreferences":"Unenclosed backreferences support at most three digits, so a sequence such as \\1000 is parsed as \\100 followed by a literal 0 even when enough capturing groups exist. Enclosed backreferences support arbitrary digit counts, while a separate vscode-oniguruma bug reportedly prevents patterns with more than 999 captures from matching.","Erroring on patterns that trigger Oniguruma bugs":"The library intentionally avoids reproducing Oniguruma bugs and currently rejects several problematic edge cases. Expandable explanations cover nested absence functions, bare \\x, incomplete \\u, and invalid standalone encoded bytes, with some future changes planned to follow valid Oniguruma behavior without reproducing bugs.","🔢 Oniguruma version":"All library versions to date follow Oniguruma 6.9.10, released January 1, 2025, with Unicode 16.0.0. Syntax changes have generally been backward compatible since at least Oniguruma 6.0.0, and the differences from the 6.9.8 version used by recent vscode-oniguruma releases are described as negligible.","🧩 Contributing":"Contributions to the project are welcome. A linked contribution guide explains how to get started.","🏷️ About":"The project was created by Steven Levithan and contributors. The section recognizes supporters, invites contributions, sharing, and sponsorship, and states that the project is 