{"sections":{"CSSType":"CSSType provides TypeScript and Flow definitions generated from MDN data for CSS property and value autocompletion and type checking. Its introductory examples show invalid property names and values being rejected, and subsequent examples use TypeScript.","Getting started":"Install the package with `npm install csstype`. This makes its CSS type definitions available to your project.","Table of content":"The table of contents links to the style, at-rule, pseudo, generics, usage, troubleshooting, version 3.0, and contributing sections. It does not include links to the version 3.1 or 3.2 sections or the Commands subsection.","At-rule types":"Descriptor interfaces are provided for `@counter-style`, `@font-face`, and `@viewport`, with default, hyphenated, fallback, and hyphenated-fallback variants. TypeScript exposes them through the `AtRule` namespace, while Flow uses an `AtRule$` prefix.","Pseudo types":"`Pseudos` combines string literal types for pseudo classes and pseudo elements through `AdvancedPseudos` and `SimplePseudos`. Advanced pseudos represent function-like selector names without their arguments or parentheses, while simple pseudos represent fixed selectors such as `:hover`.","Generics":"Interfaces accept optional length and time type parameters, defaulting to `string | 0` and `string` respectively. These parameters can be customized to permit numeric lengths or times for platforms and libraries that support them.","Usage":"Examples demonstrate typed style objects, array-valued fallbacks, and mappings from pseudo selectors to styles. They also show combining camel-cased and hyphenated property interfaces and assigning a typed style object to an HTML element's style.","What should I do when I get type errors?":"Check whether the CSS is valid and supported, consider TypeScript literal widening, and search or file an issue when definitions appear incorrect. For local fixes or custom properties, use TypeScript module augmentation preferably, or type assertions in TypeScript or Flow.","Version 3.2":"Version 3.2 is no longer compatible with version 2, and installing both can produce conflicts. An npm `overrides` entry forcing `csstype` to `^3.2.0` is suggested as a potential fix.","Version 3.1":"Version 3.1 exposes CSS data types. These are accessed as `DataType.Color` in TypeScript and `DataType$Color` in Flow.","Version 3.0":"Version 3.0 introduces namespaced property and at-rule types, leaves data types unexposed, and adds a time generic and stricter, exact Flow types. It also uses string and number intersection tricks to preserve autocompletion, with `PropertyValue<T>` available to unpack those types.","Contributing":"Do not edit `index.d.ts` or `index.js.flow` directly, because these generated files are committed to track resulting changes. After forking and cloning, run `git config merge.ours.driver true` to prevent merge conflicts during rebases.","Commands":"Use `npm run build` to generate and type-check typings, and `npm run watch` to rebuild on each save. Use `npm run test` to run tests and `npm run lazy` to type-check, lint, and format everything."},"concerns":[],"rationale":"Read README.md and summarized every heading-defined section in exactly two sentences, excluding Style types as assigned to another worker."}

